Here is an example code using NLTK library:
```
#include <iostream>
#include <string>
#include <vector>
#include <nltk/tokenize/punkt.h>
#include <nltk/tokenize/treebank.h>
#include <nltk/tag.h>
int main() {
  std::string text = "The fireman quickly put out the raging fire";
  nltk::PunktLanguageVars punkt_vars;
  punkt_vars.train(text);
  nltk::TreebankLanguageVars treebank_vars;
  nltk::Tagger tagger(treebank_vars);
  std::vector<std::string> sentences = punkt_vars.sent_tokenize(text);
  for (const auto& sentence : sentences) {
    std::vector<std::string> words = treebank_vars.word_tokenize(sentence);
    std::vector<std::pair<std::string, std::string>> pos_tags = tagger.tag(words);
    for (int i = 0; i < words.size(); i++) {
      std::cout << "Word: " << words[i] << ", POS: " << pos_tags[i].second << std::endl;
    }
  }
  return 0;
}
```
This code uses the NLTK library to tokenize the sentence and identify the part of speech for each word. The code first trains the Punkt tokenizer using the input text, and then uses it to split the text into sentences. The Treebank tokenizer is then used to tokenize each sentence into words, and the NLTK tagger is used to identify the part of speech for each word. The resulting words and their associated POS tags are printed to the console.

