Here is an example Scala program that can arrange a set of identical blocks into a perfect square formation with the given constraints:
```scala
object SquareFormation {
  def main(args: Array[String]): Unit = {
    val blocks = Array(1, 2, 3, 4, 5, 6, 7, 8, 9)
    val n = blocks.length
    val sum = blocks.sum / 3 // since each row and column must have the same sum, the total sum must be divisible by 3
    val square = Array.ofDim[Int](n, n)
    var i = 0
    var j = n / 2
    for (k <- 1 to n * n) {
      square(i)(j) = blocks(k - 1)
      i -= 1
      j += 1
      if (i < 0 && j < n) {
        i = n - 1
      } else if (j == n && i >= 0) {
        j = 0
      } else if (square(i)(j) != 0) {
        i += 1
        j -= 2
      }
    }
    for (i <- 0 until n) {
      for (j <- 0 until n) {
        print(square(i)(j) + " ")
      }
      println()
    }
  }
}
```
This program uses a modified version of the "Knight's Tour" algorithm to fill in the square. The `blocks` array contains the positive integers from 1 to 9, and `n` is the length of the array. The `sum` variable is calculated by dividing the sum of the integers in the `blocks` array by 3, since each row and column must have the same sum.
The `square` array is a 2D array that will hold the final square formation. The algorithm starts by placing the highest number (9) in the center of the first row. It then moves diagonally up and to the right, filling in the remaining numbers in a clockwise spiral pattern. If the algorithm encounters a previously filled cell, it backtracks and moves down one row and two columns to the left.
The resulting square formation should meet the given constraints. Additionally, since the algorithm starts with the highest number in the center of the first row, the highest numbers will be placed in the center of the square.

